    "observations": [
     {
      "index": 0,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "library.py:60-72 confirms filter_books calls scan_folder, populates file_list with walked paths, then filters with the unescaped regex \\.epub|\\.mobi|\\.pdf; no content hash or work-level key is computed in that path."
     },
     {
      "index": 1,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "library.py:309-328 confirms compare_shelf_current obtains stored Book.file_name values via Storage.book_paths_list and returns the set difference between on-disk and stored paths, so the upstream suppression is path-based rather than content-based."
     },
     {
      "index": 2,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "library.py:114-123 confirms the no-dc:title fallback takes the final path component and removes the extension, then applies the Book-prefix regex split before use."
     },
     {
      "index": 3,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "storage.py:277-295 confirms fields title, author, and tags, field:value and field:quoted-value parsing, and a keywords bucket for non-field bare/quoted tokens; unknown field prefixes are silently discarded upstream, which the target design deliberately replaces with 422 unknown_field."
     },
     {
      "index": 4,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "storage.py:297-332 confirms case-insensitive substring matching for requested fields via match_field, then rapidfuzz process.extract with fuzz.WRatio and a score > 50 filter for keyword queries."
     },
     {
      "index": 5,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "storage.py:68-111 confirms insert_book uses one SQLAlchemy Session per book, reads cover bytes from book[2].data or book[2], commits, and logs and returns False when an exception occurs."
     },
     {
      "index": 6,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "models.py:19-42 confirms the Book model stores one file_name string plus title, optional author, cover bytes, tags, categories, and a book_collections relationship to BookCollection."
     },
     {
      "index": 7,
      "verdict": "accepted",
      "reason": "README.md:21-36 confirms the documented feature list: recursive scanning, cover aggregation, fuzzy search with tag/author/title specifiers, download, folder-derived collections, and epub/mobi support."
     }
    ],
    "issues": [
     "Entry removal is omitted even though the leaf lists it as defining core: leaf.md:163-169 explicitly includes 'removal of entries' under 'Defining core — without these, not an e-book library', while the architecture §15 (VIEW_ARCH.md:166-168) and the authored gap (REVIEW_SCOPE.md:136-139, AUTHOR_INPUT.json:94) deliberately exclude removal. The fixed scan/correct/collection/search/download scenario does not exercise removal, but the template is presented as a core-usable e-book library and its own gap acknowledges the conflict. Explicit disclosure does not satisfy the leaf core. Add a minimal removal contract and acceptance (at least catalog-only removal, or entry-plus-file removal with a documented file policy), or explicitly narrow the template to a scenario slice and stop claiming the full leaf core."
    ],
    "summary": "The scenario specification and probe are strong: A01-A04 cover scan, covers, duplicate suppression, failure isolation, revision conflict, correction, collection assignment, restart durability, field-qualified search with negative and unsupported-field cases, and exact-byte file handoff. All eight upstream observations match the fixed source bytes, and the Node/Fastify/SQLite design is clearly separated from the GPL pyShelf evidence with no execution or certification claims. One blocking scope defect remains: the template omits entry removal, which leaf.md lists as defining core, and only records that omission as a gap. The template is not core-usable as claimed until a minimal removal path and acceptance are added, or its scope is explicitly narrowed."
   },
